### Probably related to

- #70 
- #72 
- #128 

#### Description

Profiling with **Sysprof** reveals the "smoking gun" behind the performance degradation: the vast majority of CPU resource consumption inside the `gnome-shell` process is concentrated on **`js::NativeGetProperty`**.

Enabling or initialising **Copyous** triggers a ~2s GNOME Shell freeze and forces the NVIDIA discrete GPU out of runtime suspend (RTD3) into an active state. Rather than heavy logical processing or mathematical loops, the performance hit stems from excessive, synchronous cross-language communication between JavaScript and C during the eager loading of history items.

---

#### Technical Root Cause

* **The JS-to-C Bridge:** GNOME Shell runs on SpiderMonkey (Mozilla's JavaScript engine), whereas visual components (Clutter, St, Pango) are implemented in C. Every time an extension script queries a widget's property (e.g., checking `width`, reading `text`, or verifying `visible`), the engine must cross the bridge via `js::NativeGetProperty` to extract the value from the native C object.
* **Single-Threaded Blocking:** While individual lookups take only fractions of a millisecond, eagerly mounting a large history list creates a severe compounding effect. If a user has hundreds of items stored, iterating through them and querying multiple native properties per item triggers hundreds of bridge crossings concurrently. Because GNOME Shell operates on a single-threaded event loop, the entire interface freezes, dropping frames while waiting for these property lookups to finish.

---

#### Code References

1. **Mass Initialisation Loop (`extension.ts`)**
   The extension clears and iterates through the entire database history upon initialisation, instantly invoking `addEntry()` for every single record.


```typescript
// extension.ts
private async initEntryTracker() {
    if (!this.entryTracker || !this.entryTracker.shouldInit) return;

    this.clipboardDialog?.clearEntries();
    const entries = await this.entryTracker.init();
    for (const entry of entries) {
        this.clipboardDialog?.addEntry(entry); // Triggers massive JS-to-C bridge lookups in a tight loop
    }
}

```


2. **Eager Component Instantiation (`clipboardDialog.ts`)**
   Every clipboard entry type is fully instantiated into clutter actors upfront without virtualisation or lazy loading, maximizing property overhead during startup.


```typescript
// clipboardDialog.ts
public addEntry(entry: ClipboardEntry): void {
    let item;
    try {
        item = (() => {
            switch (entry.type) {
                case ItemType.Text: return new TextItem(this.ext, entry);
                case ItemType.Code: return new CodeItem(this.ext, entry);
                // Eagerly instantiates all items, forcing immediate property evaluation
                default: return null;
            }
        })();
        // ...
        this._scrollView.addItem(item);
    } catch (e) {
        // ...
    }
}

```



---

#### Impact on Hybrid Systems (Intel + NVIDIA RTD3)

* **GPU Wake-Up:** The massive allocation spike and rendering pressure force Mutter/GNOME Shell to spin up contexts that trigger the proprietary NVIDIA driver, preventing the dGPU from entering or staying in deep RTD3 sleep.
* **UI Latency:** The single-threaded event loop blocks completely while processing the heavy queue of `js::NativeGetProperty` calls.

#### Workaround 

While a virtualised list (lazy loading or actor recycling) is not implemented, so that only the items visible within the viewport are instantiated and queried, as a user, like i have experieced my self, limit the saved history size in the extension preferences to a smaller number (e.g., 15–20 items) to drastically reduce the number of `js::NativeGetProperty` crossings during initialisation.

### Problem



## Steps to Reproduce

1. Open a terminal (Electron-based, e.g. VS Code / Cursor integrated terminal)
2. Have some text in clipboard history
3. Open Copyous dialog (click panel icon or `Shift+Super+V`)
4. Click on a clipboard entry
5. **Expected**: Text is pasted into the terminal
6. **Actual**: Text is copied to clipboard but not pasted. Manual `Ctrl+Shift+V` is required.

## Root Cause

Two issues combine to cause this:

### 1. Paste keystrokes use `Insert`-based shortcuts

`lib/misc/clipboard.js` sends `Shift+Insert` (non-terminal) or `Ctrl+Shift+Insert` (terminal) to simulate paste. Many modern terminals — especially Electron-based ones (VS Code, Cursor, Warp) — do not respond to `Insert`-based paste shortcuts. They only support `Ctrl+V` / `Ctrl+Shift+V`.

### 2. Terminal detection lost during modal

The extension detects terminals via `Main.inputMethod.content_purpose === TERMINAL`. When the Copyous dialog opens with `pushModal`, the terminal loses IME focus, resetting `content_purpose` to `NORMAL`. By the time the paste keystroke fires (250ms later), the extension thinks it's pasting into a non-terminal app.

Additionally, Electron-based terminals don't report `InputContentPurpose.TERMINAL` via the Wayland input method protocol at all, so the terminal path is never taken regardless.


### Description

## Environment
- **GNOME Shell**: 50.1
- **Mutter**: 18 (libmutter-18-0 50.1-0ubuntu2)
- **Session**: Wayland
- **OS**: Ubuntu 26.04 LTS
- **Copyous version**: 2.0.0
- **Terminal**: Cursor IDE integrated terminal (Electron-based)

## Description

When selecting a clipboard entry from the Copyous dialog, the text is copied to the clipboard but **not auto-pasted** into the focused application. This affects terminals specifically — pasting works in text editors and browsers.

### Alternative Solutions


## Proposed Fix

### File 1: `lib/misc/keyboard.js`

Add purpose save/restore to preserve terminal detection across modal:

```diff
 export class Keyboard {
     _device;
     _purpose = Clutter.InputContentPurpose.NORMAL;
+    _savedPurpose = null;

     // ... constructor unchanged ...

     get purpose() {
-        return this._purpose;
+        return this._savedPurpose ?? this._purpose;
     }

+    savePurpose() {
+        this._savedPurpose = this._purpose;
+    }
+
+    restorePurpose() {
+        this._savedPurpose = null;
+    }
+
     // ... rest unchanged ...
 }
```

### File 2: `lib/misc/clipboard.js`

Change paste keystrokes from `Insert`-based to `V`-based:

```diff
     pasteContent(content) {
         this.copyContent(content);
         if (this.pasteSignalId >= 0) GLib.source_remove(this.pasteSignalId);
-        this.pasteSignalId = GLib.timeout_add(GLib.PRIORITY_DEFAULT, 250, () => {
-            // https://github.com/Tudmotu/gnome-shell-extension-clipboard-indicator/blob/...
+        this.pasteSignalId = GLib.timeout_add(GLib.PRIORITY_DEFAULT, 300, () => {
             if (this.keyboard.purpose === Clutter.InputContentPurpose.TERMINAL) {
                 this.keyboard.press(Clutter.KEY_Control_L);
                 this.keyboard.press(Clutter.KEY_Shift_L);
-                this.keyboard.press(Clutter.KEY_Insert);
-                this.keyboard.release(Clutter.KEY_Insert);
+                this.keyboard.press(Clutter.KEY_v);
+                this.keyboard.release(Clutter.KEY_v);
                 this.keyboard.release(Clutter.KEY_Shift_L);
                 this.keyboard.release(Clutter.KEY_Control_L);
             } else {
-                this.keyboard.press(Clutter.KEY_Shift_L);
-                this.keyboard.press(Clutter.KEY_Insert);
-                this.keyboard.release(Clutter.KEY_Insert);
-                this.keyboard.release(Clutter.KEY_Shift_L);
+                this.keyboard.press(Clutter.KEY_Control_L);
+                this.keyboard.press(Clutter.KEY_v);
+                this.keyboard.release(Clutter.KEY_v);
+                this.keyboard.release(Clutter.KEY_Control_L);
             }
+            this.keyboard.restorePurpose();
             this.pasteSignalId = -1;
             return GLib.SOURCE_REMOVE;
         });
     }
```

### File 3: `extension.js`

Save keyboard purpose when dialog opens:

```diff
         this.clipboardDialog.connect('notify::opened', async () => {
+            if (this.clipboardDialog?.opened) {
+                this.clipboardManager?.keyboard.savePurpose();
+            }
             // Update the history when the dialog is closed ...
```

## Notes

- `Ctrl+V` is universally supported in GTK, Qt, and Electron apps
- `Ctrl+Shift+V` is the modern terminal paste standard (GNOME Terminal, Tilix, Kitty, Alacritty, Electron terminals)
- The `Shift+Insert` approach was inherited from [gnome-shell-extension-clipboard-indicator](https://github.com/Tudmotu/gnome-shell-extension-clipboard-indicator) which targeted X11 and older GNOME versions
- Timeout bumped from 250ms to 300ms to give Wayland compositor more time to refocus the target window after `popModal`


